Improving Our Search Algorithm

Given a constraint: datadep(X,Y,V)

With: X1...Xn (domain of X)
      Y1...Yn (domain of Y)
      V (any variable in program)

How can we evaluate it efficiently?

An improvement:

 *  Assume at some point in the CSP, we are given a particular instance 
    of X, Xi (by definition, a producer of data values), that produces
    a single variable Vk (e.g., an assignment, but also any arithmetic
    operator, like Plus, Minus, and so on).

    Further assume that the statements following any producer in the
    data flow graph are organized by type:

       (a1,...,an; b1,...,bn, etc...)

    The type A's are a1...an, the type B's are b1...bn, and so on.

    Then we can in order "1" check whether there are any Y's that
    potentially follow Xi (and since all the Y's that follow Xi in
    the data flow graph by definition use the same variable Xi
    produces, we are checking whether there are any Y's that use 
    the value of Vk produced by Xi).   

    If there are no Y's, we can immediately eliminate Xi without 
    evaluating the individual datadep(Xi, Y, Vi) for all instances of Y!
 
    An example:
       Suppose we have an ASSIGN(a,b) and a general datadep between 
       an ASSIGN and a PRINT.  If this *PARTICULAR* assign does not
       produce a variable (a) used by some PRINT, we don't have to
       check any of the PRINTs.     

This requires:

 * The CSP prefer the "in" node of a data-dep constraint to the
   "out" node
    
 * The CSP has a time when it selects Xi for cross product of 
   Y's (rather than choosing to do individual Xi*Yj evaluations
   at different times)

What about the flip side?

 *  Assume at some point in the CSP, we are given a particular instance 
    of Y, Yi (by definition, a consumer of data values), that uses
    a set of variables Vk..Vm (e.g., an expression - except for things
    like function calls, we can assume no more than 2 variables into
    a statement, and in general the average is probably right around 2)
    
    Suppose we assume that the statements preceding any consumer in the
    data flow graph are organized by type:

       (a1,...,an; b1,...,bn, etc...)

    The type A's are a1...an, the type B's are b1...bn, and so on.
    
    Then we can in order "c" check whether there are any X's that
    potentially precede Yi.

    If there are no X's, we can immediately eliminate Yi without 
    evaluating the individual datadep(X, Y, Vi) for all instances
    of X!   (Since all the X's precede Yi in the data flow graph
    produce one of the variables Yi is using, we are checking
    whether there are any X's that produce any of the variables
    consumed by X.)

    An example:
       Suppose we have an PLUS(a,b,c) and a general datadep between 
       an MULTIPLY and the PLUS.  If neither a or b of this *PARTICULAR* 
       PLUS are produced are by a MULTIPLE, we don't have to check
       of the MULTIPLIES.     

This relaxes the requirement that the CSP prefer the "in" node
of a data-dep constraint to the "out" node.  It should be
acceptable regardless of whether an "x" is picked or a "y".

However, it now adds the requirement that:

   The CSP has a time when it selects Yi for cross product of 
   X's (rather than choosing to do individual Xi*Yj evaluations
   at different times)

